window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-114"]=[{"id":"K114.01","q":"114:1–6","s":114,"surah":"al-Nās","surah_ar":"الناس","title":"al-Nās · Sūrat al-Nās","cairo":"K IV:823–825 · T XVI:652–656","source_page_first":823,"source_page_last":825,"chunk":"passages-114","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>Sūrat al-Nās. There is disagreement about it [whether Meccan or Medinan]; it has six verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of humankind, the King of humankind, the God of humankind, from the evil of the whisperer who withdraws, who whispers in the breasts of humankind, from among jinn and humankind.” [114:1–6]\n[TEXT VARIANT: The separate four-volume transcription calls it Meccan, also said to be Medinan, adds “revealed after al-Falaq,” and repeats the basmala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">بسملة</bdi>). Dubai&#39;s base heading is translated above.]</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K114.01"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"مختلف فيها، وهي ست آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\n﴿قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ النَّاسِ (1) مَلِكِ النَّاسِ (2) إِلهِ النَّاسِ (3) مِنْ شَرِّ الْوَسْواسِ الْخَنَّاسِ (4) الَّذِي يُوَسْوِسُ فِي صُدُورِ النَّاسِ (5) مِنَ الْجِنَّةِ وَالنَّاسِ﴾ [1 - 6]","layers":[{"id":"K114.01","heading":"[K114.01 | T XVI652; K IV823]","text":"Sūrat al-Nās. There is disagreement about it [whether Meccan or Medinan]; it has six verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of humankind, the King of humankind, the God of humankind, from the evil of the whisperer who withdraws, who whispers in the breasts of humankind, from among jinn and humankind.” [114:1–6]\n[TEXT VARIANT: The separate four-volume transcription calls it Meccan, also said to be Medinan, adds “revealed after al-Falaq,” and repeats the basmala (بسملة). Dubai's base heading is translated above.]","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":114,"ar":"مختلف فيها، وهي ست آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\n﴿قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ النَّاسِ (1) مَلِكِ النَّاسِ (2) إِلهِ النَّاسِ (3) مِنْ شَرِّ الْوَسْواسِ الْخَنَّاسِ (4) الَّذِي يُوَسْوِسُ فِي صُدُورِ النَّاسِ (5) مِنَ الْجِنَّةِ وَالنَّاسِ﴾ [1 - 6]","html":"<p>Sūrat al-Nās. There is disagreement about it [whether Meccan or Medinan]; it has six verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of humankind, the King of humankind, the God of humankind, from the evil of the whisperer who withdraws, who whispers in the breasts of humankind, from among jinn and humankind.” [114:1–6]\n[TEXT VARIANT: The separate four-volume transcription calls it Meccan, also said to be Medinan, adds “revealed after al-Falaq,” and repeats the basmala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">بسملة</bdi>). Dubai&#39;s base heading is translated above.]</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true,"display_normalization":"Reversed electronic reference brackets normalized; source text preserved in the dossier."}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"114:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلنَّاسِ","en":"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind"},{"key":"114:2","number":2,"ar":"مَلِكِ ٱلنَّاسِ","en":"The King of mankind"},{"key":"114:3","number":3,"ar":"إِلَـٰهِ ٱلنَّاسِ","en":"The god of mankind"},{"key":"114:4","number":4,"ar":"مِن شَرِّ ٱلْوَسْوَاسِ ٱلْخَنَّاسِ","en":"From the evil of the sneaking whisperer"},{"key":"114:5","number":5,"ar":"ٱلَّذِى يُوَسْوِسُ فِى صُدُورِ ٱلنَّاسِ","en":"Who whispereth in the hearts of mankind"},{"key":"114:6","number":6,"ar":"مِنَ ٱلْجِنَّةِ وَٱلنَّاسِ","en":"Of the jinn and of mankind"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلنَّاسِ ﴿1﴾ مَلِكِ ٱلنَّاسِ ﴿2﴾ إِلَـٰهِ ٱلنَّاسِ ﴿3﴾ مِن شَرِّ ٱلْوَسْوَاسِ ٱلْخَنَّاسِ ﴿4﴾ ٱلَّذِى يُوَسْوِسُ فِى صُدُورِ ٱلنَّاسِ ﴿5﴾ مِنَ ٱلْجِنَّةِ وَٱلنَّاسِ ﴿6﴾","quran_en":"1. Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind 2. The King of mankind 3. The god of mankind 4. From the evil of the sneaking whisperer 5. Who whispereth in the hearts of mankind 6. Of the jinn and of mankind"},{"id":"K114.02","q":"114:1","s":114,"surah":"al-Nās","surah_ar":"الناس","title":"al-Nās · “Qul aʿūdhu” has been read with the hamza omitted and its vowel transferred to the lām","cairo":"K IV:823–825 · T XVI:652–656","source_page_first":823,"source_page_last":825,"chunk":"passages-114","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>“Qul aʿūdhu” has been read with the hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) omitted and its vowel transferred to the lām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ل</bdi>). Comparable is “fa-khudh arbaʿata (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أربعة</bdi>)” [2:260].\nIf you ask, “Why say ‘in the Lord of humankind,’ annexing Lord specifically to them?”, I answer: because refuge is sought from the evil of the one who whispers in human breasts. It is as though one had said: I seek refuge from the evil of the one who whispers to people, in their Lord who controls their affairs, who is their God and the one they worship—just as some dependants, when a calamity befalls them, seek help from their master, the one they serve, and the governor of their affairs.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K114.02"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“Why say ‘in the Lord of humankind’?”","html":"<p>Sūrat al-Nās. Meccan; also said to be Medinan. Six verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\nHis words, “Why say ‘in the Lord of humankind’?”: that is, He is the Lord of all worlds, so why single out humankind here? He answers: it is humankind alone who seek help from their Lord, owner and object of worship against the affliction that befalls them.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T114.01"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“Just as some dependants, when a calamity befalls them, seek help from their master, the one they serve, and the governor of their affairs”","html":"<p>His words, “Just as some dependants, when a calamity befalls them, seek help from their master, the one they serve, and the governor of their affairs.” He observes an ascent in the help sought: protection through governance is stronger than through being served, while protection through mastery is weaker than through being served. Likewise, the sense of irresistible dominion in divinity is higher than in ownership, and [that is higher] than in lordship.\nSome commentaries say: “Repelling Satan&#39;s evil and whispering is through one of three things: either not enabling him to whisper, inasmuch as He is Lord; or enabling him but forcibly preventing him, inasmuch as He is King; or forbidding him to whisper as a rebuke, yet willing it by choice, inasmuch as He is God.”\nAlternatively, it may be said: the servant seeks refuge in God from Satan, grounding the request in appropriate attributes in ascending order. He first describes Him—mighty and majestic—as Lord, because the first thing the servant knows of his Lord is that He bestows blessings upon his outward and inward being. He then advances to knowing that He governs him and owns him, and then to knowing that He alone is the object of worship without qualification and that there is no final resort except to Him.\n[REVIEW T114-U2: The difficult third alternative reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لكن يريدها اختيارا</bdi>. “Yet willing it by choice” preserves the wording; the subject and the intended theological distinction between prohibition and willing need checking in the unnamed source. Do not replace it with “the devil chooses it” without evidence.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T114.02"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"قرئ: (قل أعوذ) بحذف الهمزة ونقل حركتها إلى اللام، ونحوه: فخذ أربعة.\n\nفإن قلت: لم قيل (بِرَبِّ النَّاسِ) مضافا إليهم خاصة؟\n\nقلت: لأنّ الاستعاذة وقعت من شر الموسوس في صدور الناس، فكأنه قيل: أعوذ من شر الموسوس إلى الناس بربهم الذي يملك عليهم أمورهم، وهو إلههم ومعبودهم، كما يستغيث بعض الموالي إذا اعتراهم خطب بسيدهم ومخدومهم ووالي أمرهم.\n\nمكية، وقيل: مدنية، وهي ست آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقولُه: (لِمَ قيل: ﴿بِرَبِّ النَّاسِ﴾)، أَيْ أنه ربُّ جميعِ العالمين، فلِمَ خُصَّ بالناسِ ها هنا؟ وأجابَ: إن المستغيثَ هو الناسُ وحدَه إلى ربِّه ومالكِه ومعبودِه، مما يُصيبُه من البلاء.\n\nقولُه: (كما يستغيثُ بعضُ الموالي إذا اعتراهم خَطْبٌ بسيّدهم ومخدومِهم ووالي أمرِهم)، راعى فيه الترقّي في الإغاثة؛ فإن الدّفعَ من جهةِ التوليةِ أقوى من جهةِ الخدمة، ثم من\n\nجهة السيادة أضعفُ من جهةِ الخدمة. كذلك معنى القَهّاريةِ في الألوهيةِ أعلى منه من معنى المالكيّة، ثم من جهةِ الرّبيّة.\n\nوفي بعض التفاسير: إنَّ دَفْعَ شرِّ الشيطانِ ووسوستِه بأحدِ أمورً ثلاثة، إمّا بأن لا يُمكِّنُه من الوسوسةِ من حيثُ كونُه ربًّا، أو بأنْ يُمكِّنُه، لكن يمنعُه قهرًا من حيثُ المالكية، أو بأن ينهاه عن الوسوسةِ زجرًا، لكن يريدُها اختيارًا من حيثُ كونُه إلهًا، أو يُقال: إن العبدَ استعاذَ بالله من الشيطان.\n\nوعَلّلَ الاستعاذةَ بأوصافٍ مناسبةٍ على الترقي: وَصْفُه عز وجل أولًا بأنه الرّبُّ، لأن أول ما يَعرفُ العبدُ من ربِّه، كونُه منعِمًا عليه ظاهرِه وباطنِه، ثم ينتقلُ منه إلى المعرفةِ بأنه متصرفٌ فيه ومالكُه، ثم ينتقلُ إلى المعرفةِ بأنه هو المعبودُ على الإطلاق، وأنْ لا مصيرَ إلا إليه.","layers":[{"id":"K114.02","heading":"[K114.02 | T XVI652; K IV823]","text":"“Qul aʿūdhu” has been read with the hamza (همزة) omitted and its vowel transferred to the lām (ل). Comparable is “fa-khudh arbaʿata (أربعة)” [2:260].\nIf you ask, “Why say ‘in the Lord of humankind,’ annexing Lord specifically to them?”, I answer: because refuge is sought from the evil of the one who whispers in human breasts. It is as though one had said: I seek refuge from the evil of the one who whispers to people, in their Lord who controls their affairs, who is their God and the one they worship—just as some dependants, when a calamity befalls them, seek help from their master, the one they serve, and the governor of their affairs.","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":114,"ar":"قرئ: (قل أعوذ) بحذف الهمزة ونقل حركتها إلى اللام، ونحوه: فخذ أربعة.\n\nفإن قلت: لم قيل (بِرَبِّ النَّاسِ) مضافا إليهم خاصة؟\n\nقلت: لأنّ الاستعاذة وقعت من شر الموسوس في صدور الناس، فكأنه قيل: أعوذ من شر الموسوس إلى الناس بربهم الذي يملك عليهم أمورهم، وهو إلههم ومعبودهم، كما يستغيث بعض الموالي إذا اعتراهم خطب بسيدهم ومخدومهم ووالي أمرهم.","html":"<p>“Qul aʿūdhu” has been read with the hamza (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">همزة</bdi>) omitted and its vowel transferred to the lām (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ل</bdi>). Comparable is “fa-khudh arbaʿata (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">أربعة</bdi>)” [2:260].\nIf you ask, “Why say ‘in the Lord of humankind,’ annexing Lord specifically to them?”, I answer: because refuge is sought from the evil of the one who whispers in human breasts. It is as though one had said: I seek refuge from the evil of the one who whispers to people, in their Lord who controls their affairs, who is their God and the one they worship—just as some dependants, when a calamity befalls them, seek help from their master, the one they serve, and the governor of their affairs.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T114.01","heading":"[T114.01 | XVI652 | on K114.02]","text":"Sūrat al-Nās. Meccan; also said to be Medinan. Six verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\nHis words, “Why say ‘in the Lord of humankind’?”: that is, He is the Lord of all worlds, so why single out humankind here? He answers: it is humankind alone who seek help from their Lord, owner and object of worship against the affliction that befalls them.","title":"“Why say ‘in the Lord of humankind’?”","author":"tibi","on":["K114.02"],"anchor":"K114.02","surah":114,"ar":"مكية، وقيل: مدنية، وهي ست آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقولُه: (لِمَ قيل: ﴿بِرَبِّ النَّاسِ﴾)، أَيْ أنه ربُّ جميعِ العالمين، فلِمَ خُصَّ بالناسِ ها هنا؟ وأجابَ: إن المستغيثَ هو الناسُ وحدَه إلى ربِّه ومالكِه ومعبودِه، مما يُصيبُه من البلاء.","html":"<p>Sūrat al-Nās. Meccan; also said to be Medinan. Six verses.\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\nHis words, “Why say ‘in the Lord of humankind’?”: that is, He is the Lord of all worlds, so why single out humankind here? He answers: it is humankind alone who seek help from their Lord, owner and object of worship against the affliction that befalls them.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T114.02","heading":"[T114.02 | XVI652–653 | on K114.02]","text":"His words, “Just as some dependants, when a calamity befalls them, seek help from their master, the one they serve, and the governor of their affairs.” He observes an ascent in the help sought: protection through governance is stronger than through being served, while protection through mastery is weaker than through being served. Likewise, the sense of irresistible dominion in divinity is higher than in ownership, and [that is higher] than in lordship.\nSome commentaries say: “Repelling Satan's evil and whispering is through one of three things: either not enabling him to whisper, inasmuch as He is Lord; or enabling him but forcibly preventing him, inasmuch as He is King; or forbidding him to whisper as a rebuke, yet willing it by choice, inasmuch as He is God.”\nAlternatively, it may be said: the servant seeks refuge in God from Satan, grounding the request in appropriate attributes in ascending order. He first describes Him—mighty and majestic—as Lord, because the first thing the servant knows of his Lord is that He bestows blessings upon his outward and inward being. He then advances to knowing that He governs him and owns him, and then to knowing that He alone is the object of worship without qualification and that there is no final resort except to Him.\n[REVIEW T114-U2: The difficult third alternative reads لكن يريدها اختيارا. “Yet willing it by choice” preserves the wording; the subject and the intended theological distinction between prohibition and willing need checking in the unnamed source. Do not replace it with “the devil chooses it” without evidence.]","title":"“Just as some dependants, when a calamity befalls them, seek help from their master, the one they serve, and the governor of their affairs”","author":"tibi","on":["K114.02"],"anchor":"K114.02","surah":114,"ar":"قولُه: (كما يستغيثُ بعضُ الموالي إذا اعتراهم خَطْبٌ بسيّدهم ومخدومِهم ووالي أمرِهم)، راعى فيه الترقّي في الإغاثة؛ فإن الدّفعَ من جهةِ التوليةِ أقوى من جهةِ الخدمة، ثم من\n\nجهة السيادة أضعفُ من جهةِ الخدمة. كذلك معنى القَهّاريةِ في الألوهيةِ أعلى منه من معنى المالكيّة، ثم من جهةِ الرّبيّة.\n\nوفي بعض التفاسير: إنَّ دَفْعَ شرِّ الشيطانِ ووسوستِه بأحدِ أمورً ثلاثة، إمّا بأن لا يُمكِّنُه من الوسوسةِ من حيثُ كونُه ربًّا، أو بأنْ يُمكِّنُه، لكن يمنعُه قهرًا من حيثُ المالكية، أو بأن ينهاه عن الوسوسةِ زجرًا، لكن يريدُها اختيارًا من حيثُ كونُه إلهًا، أو يُقال: إن العبدَ استعاذَ بالله من الشيطان.\n\nوعَلّلَ الاستعاذةَ بأوصافٍ مناسبةٍ على الترقي: وَصْفُه عز وجل أولًا بأنه الرّبُّ، لأن أول ما يَعرفُ العبدُ من ربِّه، كونُه منعِمًا عليه ظاهرِه وباطنِه، ثم ينتقلُ منه إلى المعرفةِ بأنه متصرفٌ فيه ومالكُه، ثم ينتقلُ إلى المعرفةِ بأنه هو المعبودُ على الإطلاق، وأنْ لا مصيرَ إلا إليه.","html":"<p>His words, “Just as some dependants, when a calamity befalls them, seek help from their master, the one they serve, and the governor of their affairs.” He observes an ascent in the help sought: protection through governance is stronger than through being served, while protection through mastery is weaker than through being served. Likewise, the sense of irresistible dominion in divinity is higher than in ownership, and [that is higher] than in lordship.\nSome commentaries say: “Repelling Satan&#39;s evil and whispering is through one of three things: either not enabling him to whisper, inasmuch as He is Lord; or enabling him but forcibly preventing him, inasmuch as He is King; or forbidding him to whisper as a rebuke, yet willing it by choice, inasmuch as He is God.”\nAlternatively, it may be said: the servant seeks refuge in God from Satan, grounding the request in appropriate attributes in ascending order. He first describes Him—mighty and majestic—as Lord, because the first thing the servant knows of his Lord is that He bestows blessings upon his outward and inward being. He then advances to knowing that He governs him and owns him, and then to knowing that He alone is the object of worship without qualification and that there is no final resort except to Him.\n[REVIEW T114-U2: The difficult third alternative reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">لكن يريدها اختيارا</bdi>. “Yet willing it by choice” preserves the wording; the subject and the intended theological distinction between prohibition and willing need checking in the unnamed source. Do not replace it with “the devil chooses it” without evidence.]</p>","lemma":"كما يستغيث بعض الموالي إذا اعتراهم خطب بسيدهم ومخدومهم ووالي أمرهم","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"114:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلنَّاسِ","en":"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلنَّاسِ ﴿1﴾","quran_en":"1. Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind"},{"id":"K114.03","q":"114:1–3","s":114,"surah":"al-Nās","surah_ar":"الناس","title":"al-Nās · If you ask, “What is the relation of ‘King of humankind, God of humankind’ to ‘Lord of humankind’?”,","cairo":"K IV:823–825 · T XVI:652–656","source_page_first":823,"source_page_last":825,"chunk":"passages-114","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>If you ask, “What is the relation of ‘King of humankind, God of humankind’ to ‘Lord of humankind’?”, I answer: both are explanatory apposition (ʿaṭf bayān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عطف بيان</bdi>)), as in “the conduct of Abū Ḥafṣ, ʿUmar, al-Fārūq.” He is clarified by “King of humankind,” then further clarified by “God of humankind.” For someone other than Him may be called “lord of humankind,” as in “They took their rabbis and monks as lords apart from God” [9:31]; someone may also be called “king of humankind.” But “God of humankind” is exclusive, admitting no partnership, and is therefore made the culmination of the clarification.\nIf you ask, “Why not be content with explicitly mentioning the annexed noun ‘humankind’ once?”, I answer: because explanatory apposition serves clarification, making it a suitable place for explicit mention rather than substitution by a pronoun.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K114.03"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“Someone may also be called king of humankind”","html":"<p>His words, “Someone may also be called king of humankind.” Al-Rāghib: “A king is one who directs the populace through command and prohibition. This is peculiar to governing rational beings; hence ‘king of humankind’ is said, but not ‘king of things.’”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T114.03"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"فإن قلت: (مَلِكِ النَّاسِ إِلهِ النَّاسِ) ما هما من رب الناس؟\n\nقلت: هما عطف بيان، كقولك: سيرة أبى حفص عمر الفاروق. بين بملك الناس، ثم زيد بيانا بإله الناس، لأنه قد يقال لغيره: رب الناس، كقوله: (اتَّخَذُوا أَحْبارَهُمْ وَرُهْبانَهُمْ أَرْباباً مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ) [ التوبة: 31 ]وقد يقال: ملك الناس. وأمّا (إِلهِ النَّاسِ) فخاص لا شركة فيه، فجعل غاية للبيان.\n\nفإن قلت: فهلا اكتفى بإظهار المضاف إليه الذي هو الناس مرّة واحدة؟\n\nقلت: لأنّ عطف البيان للبيان، فكان مظنة للإظهار دون الإضمار.\n\nقولُه: (وقد يقال: مَلِكُ الناس)، الراغب: «المَلِك: هو المتصرِفُ بالأمرِ والنهيِ في الجمهور، وذلك مختصٌ بسياسةِ الناطقين؛ ولذلك يقال: مَلِكُ الناس، ولا يقال: مَلِكُ الأشياء».","layers":[{"id":"K114.03","heading":"[K114.03 | T XVI653; K IV823]","text":"If you ask, “What is the relation of ‘King of humankind, God of humankind’ to ‘Lord of humankind’?”, I answer: both are explanatory apposition (ʿaṭf bayān (عطف بيان)), as in “the conduct of Abū Ḥafṣ, ʿUmar, al-Fārūq.” He is clarified by “King of humankind,” then further clarified by “God of humankind.” For someone other than Him may be called “lord of humankind,” as in “They took their rabbis and monks as lords apart from God” [9:31]; someone may also be called “king of humankind.” But “God of humankind” is exclusive, admitting no partnership, and is therefore made the culmination of the clarification.\nIf you ask, “Why not be content with explicitly mentioning the annexed noun ‘humankind’ once?”, I answer: because explanatory apposition serves clarification, making it a suitable place for explicit mention rather than substitution by a pronoun.","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":114,"ar":"فإن قلت: (مَلِكِ النَّاسِ إِلهِ النَّاسِ) ما هما من رب الناس؟\n\nقلت: هما عطف بيان، كقولك: سيرة أبى حفص عمر الفاروق. بين بملك الناس، ثم زيد بيانا بإله الناس، لأنه قد يقال لغيره: رب الناس، كقوله: (اتَّخَذُوا أَحْبارَهُمْ وَرُهْبانَهُمْ أَرْباباً مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ) [ التوبة: 31 ]وقد يقال: ملك الناس. وأمّا (إِلهِ النَّاسِ) فخاص لا شركة فيه، فجعل غاية للبيان.\n\nفإن قلت: فهلا اكتفى بإظهار المضاف إليه الذي هو الناس مرّة واحدة؟\n\nقلت: لأنّ عطف البيان للبيان، فكان مظنة للإظهار دون الإضمار.","html":"<p>If you ask, “What is the relation of ‘King of humankind, God of humankind’ to ‘Lord of humankind’?”, I answer: both are explanatory apposition (ʿaṭf bayān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">عطف بيان</bdi>)), as in “the conduct of Abū Ḥafṣ, ʿUmar, al-Fārūq.” He is clarified by “King of humankind,” then further clarified by “God of humankind.” For someone other than Him may be called “lord of humankind,” as in “They took their rabbis and monks as lords apart from God” [9:31]; someone may also be called “king of humankind.” But “God of humankind” is exclusive, admitting no partnership, and is therefore made the culmination of the clarification.\nIf you ask, “Why not be content with explicitly mentioning the annexed noun ‘humankind’ once?”, I answer: because explanatory apposition serves clarification, making it a suitable place for explicit mention rather than substitution by a pronoun.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true,"display_normalization":"Reversed electronic reference brackets normalized; source text preserved in the dossier."}},{"id":"T114.03","heading":"[T114.03 | XVI653 | on K114.03]","text":"His words, “Someone may also be called king of humankind.” Al-Rāghib: “A king is one who directs the populace through command and prohibition. This is peculiar to governing rational beings; hence ‘king of humankind’ is said, but not ‘king of things.’”","title":"“Someone may also be called king of humankind”","author":"tibi","on":["K114.03"],"anchor":"K114.03","surah":114,"ar":"قولُه: (وقد يقال: مَلِكُ الناس)، الراغب: «المَلِك: هو المتصرِفُ بالأمرِ والنهيِ في الجمهور، وذلك مختصٌ بسياسةِ الناطقين؛ ولذلك يقال: مَلِكُ الناس، ولا يقال: مَلِكُ الأشياء».","html":"<p>His words, “Someone may also be called king of humankind.” Al-Rāghib: “A king is one who directs the populace through command and prohibition. This is peculiar to governing rational beings; hence ‘king of humankind’ is said, but not ‘king of things.’”</p>","lemma":"وقد يقال: ملك الناس","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"114:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلنَّاسِ","en":"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind"},{"key":"114:2","number":2,"ar":"مَلِكِ ٱلنَّاسِ","en":"The King of mankind"},{"key":"114:3","number":3,"ar":"إِلَـٰهِ ٱلنَّاسِ","en":"The god of mankind"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ قُلْ أَعُوذُ بِرَبِّ ٱلنَّاسِ ﴿1﴾ مَلِكِ ٱلنَّاسِ ﴿2﴾ إِلَـٰهِ ٱلنَّاسِ ﴿3﴾","quran_en":"1. Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind 2. The King of mankind 3. The god of mankind"},{"id":"K114.04","q":"114:4–5","s":114,"surah":"al-Nās","surah_ar":"الناس","title":"al-Nās · Al-waswās is a noun meaning whispering (waswasa), as zalzāl means shaking (zalzala)","cairo":"K IV:823–825 · T XVI:652–656","source_page_first":823,"source_page_last":825,"chunk":"passages-114","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>Al-waswās is a noun meaning whispering (waswasa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وسوسة</bdi>)), as zalzāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">زَلْزال</bdi>) means shaking (zalzala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">زلزلة</bdi>)). The verbal noun, however, is wiswās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وِسْواس</bdi>), with kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>), like zilzāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">زلزال</bdi>). What is meant is Satan: he is named with the verbal noun as though he were whispering itself, since that is his craft and the occupation to which he constantly devotes himself. Alternatively, “the possessor of whispering” is intended. Waswasa is a faint sound; hence waswās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وَسْواس</bdi>) al-ḥuly, the rustling of jewellery.\nAl-khannās is one whose habit is to withdraw. It is a form associating him with khunūs (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">خنوس</bdi>), retreating, like al-ʿawwāj (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">العوّاج</bdi>) and al-battāt (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">البتّات</bdi>) [occupational forms]. For it is related from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr that when a person remembers his Lord, Satan withdraws and turns away; when he is heedless, Satan whispers to him.\n“Alladhī yuwaswisu” [who whispers] may occupy a position corresponding to any of the three case vowels: genitive as a description, or nominative or accusative as an expression of invective. It is fitting for the reciter to stop at al-khannās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الخنّاس</bdi>) and begin with alladhī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الذي</bdi>) yuwaswisu on either of these two interpretations [nominative or accusative].\n[REVIEW K114-U1: “These two interpretations” naturally refers to nominative/accusative in the base passage. Ṭībī explicitly glosses it as “description and invective” below; the Turkish translation follows that gloss. The disagreement is retained rather than silently resolved.]</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K114.04"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“The verbal noun, however, is wiswās”","html":"<p>His words, “The verbal noun, however, is wiswās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وِسْواس</bdi>).” According to one explanation, by waswās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وَسْواس</bdi>) he meant the noun which has the meaning of waswasa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وسوسة</bdi>), itself the verbal noun. The Maghribī grammarians say: the difference between a verbal noun (maṣdar (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مصدر</bdi>)) and a noun of the verbal action (ism (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">اسم</bdi>) al-maṣdar (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">المصدر</bdi>)) is this. The meaning expressed by the real act—the origin of the grammatical verb—may be considered together with the agent&#39;s engagement in it, its proceeding from him, and its renewed occurrence. The expression assigned to that meaning, restricted by this qualification, is called a maṣdar (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مصدر</bdi>). If that qualification is not considered, the expression assigned to the meaning without the stated restriction is an ism (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">اسم</bdi>) al-maṣdar (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">المصدر</bdi>).\nHis words, “His craft”: another reading is “his livelihood” (ḍayʿatuhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضيعته</bdi>)). Al-Nihāya: “A man&#39;s ḍayʿa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضيعة</bdi>) is that from which he earns his living, such as a craft, trade, an industry and so on.”\nHis words, “Associated with khunūs (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">خنوس</bdi>)”: he says “associated” because withdrawing has been made his habit.\nHis words, “When a person remembers his Lord, he withdraws.” We transmitted in Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī, as a suspended report (taʿlīqan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تعليقًا</bdi>)), from Ibn ʿAbbās: the Messenger of God ﷺ said, “Satan crouches upon the heart of the son of Adam. When he remembers God, he withdraws; when he is heedless, he whispers.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T114.04"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Commentary","html":"<p>His words from “It is fitting for the reciter to stop” to “on either of these two interpretations”: that is, description and invective.\nIn al-Kawāshī: “A stop at al-khannās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الخنّاس</bdi>) is sufficient if you make [what follows] nominative or accusative in blame; it is not permissible if you make it genitive as a description of al-khannās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الخنّاس</bdi>).”\nI say: its impermissibility is questionable, considering the verse ending. The author of al-Murshid says: “When you have said ‘the All-Merciful, the Merciful,’ the stop is sufficient because it is the end of a verse. It is not a complete stop, because the genitive expression—I mean ‘Master of the Day of Judgment’—is left without its governor, and the epithet is separated from what it qualifies. Likewise a stop at ‘the straight [path]’ is permissible but not a good stop; it is permitted only because it is the end of the verse.”\n[TEXT CORRECTION T114-C1: OpenITI reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وفي عدم الجواز نظرا للفاصلة</bdi>. The scan p.654 reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وفي عدم الجواز نظر، نظرا للفاصلة</bdi>: the missing <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">نظر</bdi> is restored in this translation. “Sufficient,” “complete” and “good” preserve distinct stop categories, not interchangeable praise.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T114.05"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"(الْوَسْواسِ) اسم بمعنى الوسوسة، كالزلزال بمعنى الزلزلة، وأمّا المصدر فوسواس\n\nبالكسر كزلزال، والمراد به الشيطان، سمى بالمصدر كأنه وسوسة في نفسه؛ لأنها صنعته وشغله الذي هو عاكف عليه. أو أريد ذو الوسواس. والوسوسة: الصوت الخفي، ومنه: وسواس الحلي. و (الْخَنَّاسِ) الذي عادته أن يخنس، منسوب إلى الخنوس وهو التأخر كالعواج والبتات، لما روى عن سعيد بن جبير: إذا ذكر الإنسان ربه خنس الشيطان وولى، فإذا غفل وسوس إليه. (الَّذِي يُوَسْوِسُ) يجوز في محله الحركات الثلاث، فالجر على الصفة، والرفع والنصب على الشتم، ويحسن أن يقف القارئ على (الْخَنَّاسِ)، ويبتدئ (الَّذِي يُوَسْوِسُ) على أحد هذين الوجهين.\n\nقولُه: (وأما المصدرُ فَوِسواس)، عن بعضِهم: أراد بالوَسواسِ الاسم الذي هو بمعنى الوسوسةِ وهو المصدر. وقال المغاربةُ: الفرقُ بين المصدرِ واسمِ المصدر هو أن المعنى الذي يُعبِّرُ\n\nعنه بالفعلِ الحقيقي، الذي هو مبتدأ الفعلِ الصناعي، إذا اعتبرَ فيه تَلَبُّسُ الفاعلِ به وصدورُه منه وتَجدُّدُه؛ فاللفظُ الموضوعُ بإزائه مقيدًا بهذا القيد، سمّي مصدرًا وإن لم يعتبرْ فيه ذلك، فاللفظُ الموضوعُ بإزاءِ ذلك مطلقًا عن هذا القيدِ المذكور، هو اسمُ المصدر.\n\nقولُه: (صَنْعَتُه)، ويُرْوى: ضَيْعتُه. النهاية: «ضَيْعةُ الرجلِ: ما يكونُ منه معاشُه كالصنعةِ والتجارةِ والصناعةِ وغير ذلك».\n\nقولُه: (منسوبٌ إلى الخُنُوس)، قال: منسوبٌ من حيثُ إنه جعلَ الخنوسَ عادةً له.\n\nقولُه: (إذا ذَكرَ الإنسانُ ربَّه خَنَس)، روينا في \"صحيح البخاري\" تعليقًا عن ابنِ عباسٍ قال: قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «الشيطانُ جاثمٌ على قلبِ ابنِ آدم؛ فإذا ذَكرَ الله خَنَس، وإذا غَفلَ وَسْوس».\n\nقولُه: (ويَحْسنُ أن يقف القارئ) إلى قولُه: (على أحدِ هذين الوجهين)، أي: الصَّفةِ والشَّتْم. وفي \"الكواشي\": «يكفي الوقفُ على \"الخنّاس\" إن رفعتَ أو نصبتَ ذمًا، فلا يجوزُ إن جَرَرْتَه: صفةٌ للخناس. وقلتُ: وفي عدمِ الجوازِ نظرٌ، نظرًا للفاصلة، قالَ صاحبُ \"المرشد\": فإذا قلتَ: \"الرحمن الرحيم\"، كان الوقفُ كافيًا لأنه رأسُ آية، ولا يكونُ تامًا\n\nلخلوِّ المجرورِ، أعنى: \"مالكِ يومِ الدين\"، من العامل، والفصلِ بين النعتِ والمنعوت، وكذا الوقفُ على \"المستقيم\" جائزٌ وليس بحَسَن، وإنما جُوِّزَ لأنه آخر الآية».","layers":[{"id":"K114.04","heading":"[K114.04 | T XVI653–654; K IV823–824]","text":"Al-waswās is a noun meaning whispering (waswasa (وسوسة)), as zalzāl (زَلْزال) means shaking (zalzala (زلزلة)). The verbal noun, however, is wiswās (وِسْواس), with kasra (كَسْرة), like zilzāl (زلزال). What is meant is Satan: he is named with the verbal noun as though he were whispering itself, since that is his craft and the occupation to which he constantly devotes himself. Alternatively, “the possessor of whispering” is intended. Waswasa is a faint sound; hence waswās (وَسْواس) al-ḥuly, the rustling of jewellery.\nAl-khannās is one whose habit is to withdraw. It is a form associating him with khunūs (خنوس), retreating, like al-ʿawwāj (العوّاج) and al-battāt (البتّات) [occupational forms]. For it is related from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr that when a person remembers his Lord, Satan withdraws and turns away; when he is heedless, Satan whispers to him.\n“Alladhī yuwaswisu” [who whispers] may occupy a position corresponding to any of the three case vowels: genitive as a description, or nominative or accusative as an expression of invective. It is fitting for the reciter to stop at al-khannās (الخنّاس) and begin with alladhī (الذي) yuwaswisu on either of these two interpretations [nominative or accusative].\n[REVIEW K114-U1: “These two interpretations” naturally refers to nominative/accusative in the base passage. Ṭībī explicitly glosses it as “description and invective” below; the Turkish translation follows that gloss. The disagreement is retained rather than silently resolved.]","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":114,"ar":"(الْوَسْواسِ) اسم بمعنى الوسوسة، كالزلزال بمعنى الزلزلة، وأمّا المصدر فوسواس\n\nبالكسر كزلزال، والمراد به الشيطان، سمى بالمصدر كأنه وسوسة في نفسه؛ لأنها صنعته وشغله الذي هو عاكف عليه. أو أريد ذو الوسواس. والوسوسة: الصوت الخفي، ومنه: وسواس الحلي. و (الْخَنَّاسِ) الذي عادته أن يخنس، منسوب إلى الخنوس وهو التأخر كالعواج والبتات، لما روى عن سعيد بن جبير: إذا ذكر الإنسان ربه خنس الشيطان وولى، فإذا غفل وسوس إليه. (الَّذِي يُوَسْوِسُ) يجوز في محله الحركات الثلاث، فالجر على الصفة، والرفع والنصب على الشتم، ويحسن أن يقف القارئ على (الْخَنَّاسِ)، ويبتدئ (الَّذِي يُوَسْوِسُ) على أحد هذين الوجهين.","html":"<p>Al-waswās is a noun meaning whispering (waswasa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وسوسة</bdi>)), as zalzāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">زَلْزال</bdi>) means shaking (zalzala (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">زلزلة</bdi>)). The verbal noun, however, is wiswās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وِسْواس</bdi>), with kasra (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">كَسْرة</bdi>), like zilzāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">زلزال</bdi>). What is meant is Satan: he is named with the verbal noun as though he were whispering itself, since that is his craft and the occupation to which he constantly devotes himself. Alternatively, “the possessor of whispering” is intended. Waswasa is a faint sound; hence waswās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وَسْواس</bdi>) al-ḥuly, the rustling of jewellery.\nAl-khannās is one whose habit is to withdraw. It is a form associating him with khunūs (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">خنوس</bdi>), retreating, like al-ʿawwāj (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">العوّاج</bdi>) and al-battāt (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">البتّات</bdi>) [occupational forms]. For it is related from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr that when a person remembers his Lord, Satan withdraws and turns away; when he is heedless, Satan whispers to him.\n“Alladhī yuwaswisu” [who whispers] may occupy a position corresponding to any of the three case vowels: genitive as a description, or nominative or accusative as an expression of invective. It is fitting for the reciter to stop at al-khannās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الخنّاس</bdi>) and begin with alladhī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الذي</bdi>) yuwaswisu on either of these two interpretations [nominative or accusative].\n[REVIEW K114-U1: “These two interpretations” naturally refers to nominative/accusative in the base passage. Ṭībī explicitly glosses it as “description and invective” below; the Turkish translation follows that gloss. The disagreement is retained rather than silently resolved.]</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T114.04","heading":"[T114.04 | XVI653–654 | on K114.04]","text":"His words, “The verbal noun, however, is wiswās (وِسْواس).” According to one explanation, by waswās (وَسْواس) he meant the noun which has the meaning of waswasa (وسوسة), itself the verbal noun. The Maghribī grammarians say: the difference between a verbal noun (maṣdar (مصدر)) and a noun of the verbal action (ism (اسم) al-maṣdar (المصدر)) is this. The meaning expressed by the real act—the origin of the grammatical verb—may be considered together with the agent's engagement in it, its proceeding from him, and its renewed occurrence. The expression assigned to that meaning, restricted by this qualification, is called a maṣdar (مصدر). If that qualification is not considered, the expression assigned to the meaning without the stated restriction is an ism (اسم) al-maṣdar (المصدر).\nHis words, “His craft”: another reading is “his livelihood” (ḍayʿatuhu (ضيعته)). Al-Nihāya: “A man's ḍayʿa (ضيعة) is that from which he earns his living, such as a craft, trade, an industry and so on.”\nHis words, “Associated with khunūs (خنوس)”: he says “associated” because withdrawing has been made his habit.\nHis words, “When a person remembers his Lord, he withdraws.” We transmitted in Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī, as a suspended report (taʿlīqan (تعليقًا)), from Ibn ʿAbbās: the Messenger of God ﷺ said, “Satan crouches upon the heart of the son of Adam. When he remembers God, he withdraws; when he is heedless, he whispers.”","title":"“The verbal noun, however, is wiswās”","author":"tibi","on":["K114.04"],"anchor":"K114.04","surah":114,"ar":"قولُه: (وأما المصدرُ فَوِسواس)، عن بعضِهم: أراد بالوَسواسِ الاسم الذي هو بمعنى الوسوسةِ وهو المصدر. وقال المغاربةُ: الفرقُ بين المصدرِ واسمِ المصدر هو أن المعنى الذي يُعبِّرُ\n\nعنه بالفعلِ الحقيقي، الذي هو مبتدأ الفعلِ الصناعي، إذا اعتبرَ فيه تَلَبُّسُ الفاعلِ به وصدورُه منه وتَجدُّدُه؛ فاللفظُ الموضوعُ بإزائه مقيدًا بهذا القيد، سمّي مصدرًا وإن لم يعتبرْ فيه ذلك، فاللفظُ الموضوعُ بإزاءِ ذلك مطلقًا عن هذا القيدِ المذكور، هو اسمُ المصدر.\n\nقولُه: (صَنْعَتُه)، ويُرْوى: ضَيْعتُه. النهاية: «ضَيْعةُ الرجلِ: ما يكونُ منه معاشُه كالصنعةِ والتجارةِ والصناعةِ وغير ذلك».\n\nقولُه: (منسوبٌ إلى الخُنُوس)، قال: منسوبٌ من حيثُ إنه جعلَ الخنوسَ عادةً له.\n\nقولُه: (إذا ذَكرَ الإنسانُ ربَّه خَنَس)، روينا في \"صحيح البخاري\" تعليقًا عن ابنِ عباسٍ قال: قال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «الشيطانُ جاثمٌ على قلبِ ابنِ آدم؛ فإذا ذَكرَ الله خَنَس، وإذا غَفلَ وَسْوس».","html":"<p>His words, “The verbal noun, however, is wiswās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وِسْواس</bdi>).” According to one explanation, by waswās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وَسْواس</bdi>) he meant the noun which has the meaning of waswasa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وسوسة</bdi>), itself the verbal noun. The Maghribī grammarians say: the difference between a verbal noun (maṣdar (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مصدر</bdi>)) and a noun of the verbal action (ism (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">اسم</bdi>) al-maṣdar (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">المصدر</bdi>)) is this. The meaning expressed by the real act—the origin of the grammatical verb—may be considered together with the agent&#39;s engagement in it, its proceeding from him, and its renewed occurrence. The expression assigned to that meaning, restricted by this qualification, is called a maṣdar (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">مصدر</bdi>). If that qualification is not considered, the expression assigned to the meaning without the stated restriction is an ism (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">اسم</bdi>) al-maṣdar (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">المصدر</bdi>).\nHis words, “His craft”: another reading is “his livelihood” (ḍayʿatuhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضيعته</bdi>)). Al-Nihāya: “A man&#39;s ḍayʿa (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ضيعة</bdi>) is that from which he earns his living, such as a craft, trade, an industry and so on.”\nHis words, “Associated with khunūs (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">خنوس</bdi>)”: he says “associated” because withdrawing has been made his habit.\nHis words, “When a person remembers his Lord, he withdraws.” We transmitted in Ṣaḥīḥ al-Bukhārī, as a suspended report (taʿlīqan (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">تعليقًا</bdi>)), from Ibn ʿAbbās: the Messenger of God ﷺ said, “Satan crouches upon the heart of the son of Adam. When he remembers God, he withdraws; when he is heedless, he whispers.”</p>","lemma":"وأما المصدر فوسواس","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T114.05","heading":"[T114.05 | XVI654–655 | on K114.04]","text":"His words from “It is fitting for the reciter to stop” to “on either of these two interpretations”: that is, description and invective.\nIn al-Kawāshī: “A stop at al-khannās (الخنّاس) is sufficient if you make [what follows] nominative or accusative in blame; it is not permissible if you make it genitive as a description of al-khannās (الخنّاس).”\nI say: its impermissibility is questionable, considering the verse ending. The author of al-Murshid says: “When you have said ‘the All-Merciful, the Merciful,’ the stop is sufficient because it is the end of a verse. It is not a complete stop, because the genitive expression—I mean ‘Master of the Day of Judgment’—is left without its governor, and the epithet is separated from what it qualifies. Likewise a stop at ‘the straight [path]’ is permissible but not a good stop; it is permitted only because it is the end of the verse.”\n[TEXT CORRECTION T114-C1: OpenITI reads وفي عدم الجواز نظرا للفاصلة. The scan p.654 reads وفي عدم الجواز نظر، نظرا للفاصلة: the missing نظر is restored in this translation. “Sufficient,” “complete” and “good” preserve distinct stop categories, not interchangeable praise.]","title":"Commentary","author":"tibi","on":["K114.04"],"anchor":"K114.04","surah":114,"ar":"قولُه: (ويَحْسنُ أن يقف القارئ) إلى قولُه: (على أحدِ هذين الوجهين)، أي: الصَّفةِ والشَّتْم. وفي \"الكواشي\": «يكفي الوقفُ على \"الخنّاس\" إن رفعتَ أو نصبتَ ذمًا، فلا يجوزُ إن جَرَرْتَه: صفةٌ للخناس. وقلتُ: وفي عدمِ الجوازِ نظرٌ، نظرًا للفاصلة، قالَ صاحبُ \"المرشد\": فإذا قلتَ: \"الرحمن الرحيم\"، كان الوقفُ كافيًا لأنه رأسُ آية، ولا يكونُ تامًا\n\nلخلوِّ المجرورِ، أعنى: \"مالكِ يومِ الدين\"، من العامل، والفصلِ بين النعتِ والمنعوت، وكذا الوقفُ على \"المستقيم\" جائزٌ وليس بحَسَن، وإنما جُوِّزَ لأنه آخر الآية».","html":"<p>His words from “It is fitting for the reciter to stop” to “on either of these two interpretations”: that is, description and invective.\nIn al-Kawāshī: “A stop at al-khannās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الخنّاس</bdi>) is sufficient if you make [what follows] nominative or accusative in blame; it is not permissible if you make it genitive as a description of al-khannās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الخنّاس</bdi>).”\nI say: its impermissibility is questionable, considering the verse ending. The author of al-Murshid says: “When you have said ‘the All-Merciful, the Merciful,’ the stop is sufficient because it is the end of a verse. It is not a complete stop, because the genitive expression—I mean ‘Master of the Day of Judgment’—is left without its governor, and the epithet is separated from what it qualifies. Likewise a stop at ‘the straight [path]’ is permissible but not a good stop; it is permitted only because it is the end of the verse.”\n[TEXT CORRECTION T114-C1: OpenITI reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وفي عدم الجواز نظرا للفاصلة</bdi>. The scan p.654 reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وفي عدم الجواز نظر، نظرا للفاصلة</bdi>: the missing <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">نظر</bdi> is restored in this translation. “Sufficient,” “complete” and “good” preserve distinct stop categories, not interchangeable praise.]</p>","lemma":"ويحسن أن يقف القارئ","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"114:4","number":4,"ar":"مِن شَرِّ ٱلْوَسْوَاسِ ٱلْخَنَّاسِ","en":"From the evil of the sneaking whisperer"},{"key":"114:5","number":5,"ar":"ٱلَّذِى يُوَسْوِسُ فِى صُدُورِ ٱلنَّاسِ","en":"Who whispereth in the hearts of mankind"}],"quran_ar":"مِن شَرِّ ٱلْوَسْوَاسِ ٱلْخَنَّاسِ ﴿4﴾ ٱلَّذِى يُوَسْوِسُ فِى صُدُورِ ٱلنَّاسِ ﴿5﴾","quran_en":"4. From the evil of the sneaking whisperer 5. Who whispereth in the hearts of mankind"},{"id":"K114.05","q":"114:6","s":114,"surah":"al-Nās","surah_ar":"الناس","title":"al-Nās · “From among jinn and humankind” explains “who whispers,” on the basis that Satan is of two kinds, ji","cairo":"K IV:823–825 · T XVI:652–656","source_page_first":823,"source_page_last":825,"chunk":"passages-114","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>“From among jinn and humankind” explains “who whispers,” on the basis that Satan is of two kinds, jinn and human, as He says: “devils of humankind and jinn” [6:112]. Abū Dharr—may God be pleased with him—is reported to have asked a man: “Have you sought refuge in God from human Satan?”\nMin may also attach to yuwaswisu, denoting a starting point: that is, he whispers in their breasts from the direction of the jinn and from the direction of human beings.\nIt is also said that “from among jinn and humankind” explains al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>), and that the name al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>) applies to jinn. They adduce nafar (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">نفر</bdi>) [a group] and rijāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">رجال</bdi>) [men] in Sūrat al-Jinn as evidence. I do not accept it as established: jinn are called jinn because they are concealed, and people nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ناس</bdi>) because they are manifest, from īnās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إيناس</bdi>), seeing, just as they are called bashar. Even if nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ناس</bdi>) applied to both classes, and that were sound and established, it would not suit the Qurʾān&#39;s eloquence and its freedom from affectation.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K114.05"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“From the direction of human beings”","html":"<p>His words, “From the direction of human beings”: for example, he whispers into a Muslim&#39;s heart, concerning astrologers and soothsayers, that they know the unseen; and concerning jinn, that they harm and benefit.\nIn al-Muṭṭalaʿ: “According to one explanation, if the phrase is taken as clarification, ‘from among jinn and humankind’ is a circumstantial expression (ḥāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حال</bdi>)) relating to the pronoun in ‘who whispers.’”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T114.06"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“I do not accept it as established”","html":"<p>His words, “I do not accept it as established”: that is, I do not establish it, from their saying ḥaqaqtu al-shayʾa aḥuqquhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حَقَقْتُ الشيءَ أَحُقُّه</bdi>), “I established the thing.”\nThe Imam says: “It is said that ‘from among jinn and humankind’ gives two divisions included under ‘in the breasts of al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>).’ It is as though the common element shared by jinn and humans were called insān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إنسان</bdi>), while the human being were also called insān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إنسان</bdi>); the expression insān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إنسان</bdi>) would thus apply equivocally to both genus and species. The evidence is the report that a group of jinn came and were asked, ‘Who are you?’ They answered, ‘Nās from among the jinn.’ God has also called them rijāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">رجال</bdi>) in ‘There were men of humankind who sought refuge with men of the jinn, and they increased them in distress’ [72:6]. Thus it would be permissible to call them nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ناس</bdi>) here. This strained statement—he does not mean that it is weak—for making insān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إنسان</bdi>) the name of a genus containing both jinn and humans is remote from the language.”\n[SOURCE CHECK T114-U3: The earlier scan report gives <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وهذا القول المتعسف لا يريد أنه ضعيف</bdi>. Al-Rāzī&#39;s tafsīr of 114:6 instead reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وهذا القول ضعيف</bdi>, followed by the same linguistic objection. This confirms that the source criticizes the interpretation, but does not establish how the corruption arose in Ṭībī. The printed wording is retained with this warning; displacement from the next gloss remains a hypothesis. Mā in <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وما أحقه</bdi> is negative, not exclamatory.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T114.07"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"(مِنَ الْجِنَّةِ وَالنَّاسِ) بيان للذي يوسوس، على أن الشيطان ضربان: جني وإنسي، كما قال (شياطين الإنس والجن) [ الأنعام: 112 ]، وعن أبى ذرّ رضي الله عنه قال لرجل: هل تعوّذت بالله من شيطان الإنس؟ ويجوز أن يكون (مِنَ) متعلقا بيوسوس، ومعناه: ابتداء الغاية، أي: يوسوس في صدورهم من جهة الجنّ ومن جهة الناس، وقيل: من الجنة والناس بيان للناس، وأن اسم الناس ينطلق على الجنة، واستدلوا (بنفر) و (رجال) في سورة الجن.\n\nوما أحقه؛ لأن الجن سموا (جنا) لاجتنانهم، والناس (ناسا) لظهورهم، من الإيناس وهو الإبصار، كما سموا بشرا؛ ولو كان يقع الناس على القبيلين، وصح ذلك وثبت: لم يكن مناسبا لفصاحة القرآن وبعده من التصنع\n\nقولُه: (ومن جهة الناس)، مثلُ أن يوسوسَ في قلب المسلمِ من جهة المنجّمين والكُهانِ أنهم يعلمون الغيب، ومن جهةِ الجنِّ أنهم يَضرّون وينفعون. في \"المطلع\": «وعن بعضهم: على البيانِ يكونُ \"من الجِنّةِ والناس\"، حالًا من ضمير \"الذي يوسوس\"».\n\nقولُه: (وما أحقّه)، يعني: ما أثبته من قولهم: حَقَقْتُ الشئَ أَحُقُّه، أي: أَثبتُه. قال الإمام: «قيل: إن قولَه: ﴿مِنَ الْجِنَّةِ وَالنَّاسِ﴾ قسمان مندرجان تحت قولِه: ﴿فِي صُدُورِ النَّاسِ﴾، كأن القدرَ المشتركَ بين الجنِّ والإنسِ سُمّي إنسانًا، والإنسانُ أيضًا سُمّي إنسانًا، فيكون لفظُ الإنسانِ واقعًا على الجنسِ والنوعِ بالاشتراك. والدليلُ عليه ما رُويَ أنه جاءَ نفرٌ من الجن، فقيل لهم: مَنْ أنتم؟ فقالوا: ناسٌ من الجن.\n\nوأيضًا قد سَمّاهم اللهُ رجالًا في قوله: ﴿وَأَنَّهُ كَانَ رِجَالٌ مِنَ الْإِنْسِ يَعُوذُونَ بِرِجَالٍ مِنَ الْجِنِّ فَزَادُوهُمْ رَهَقًا﴾ [الجن: 6]، فجازَ أن يُسميَهم هنا ناسًا. وهذا القولُ المتعسِّفُ لا يريدُ أنَه ضعيفٌ، لأن جَعْلَ الإنسانِ اسمًا للجنسِ الذي يندرجُ فيه الجنُّ والإنس، بعيدٌ من اللغة».","layers":[{"id":"K114.05","heading":"[K114.05 | T XVI655; K IV824]","text":"“From among jinn and humankind” explains “who whispers,” on the basis that Satan is of two kinds, jinn and human, as He says: “devils of humankind and jinn” [6:112]. Abū Dharr—may God be pleased with him—is reported to have asked a man: “Have you sought refuge in God from human Satan?”\nMin may also attach to yuwaswisu, denoting a starting point: that is, he whispers in their breasts from the direction of the jinn and from the direction of human beings.\nIt is also said that “from among jinn and humankind” explains al-nās (الناس), and that the name al-nās (الناس) applies to jinn. They adduce nafar (نفر) [a group] and rijāl (رجال) [men] in Sūrat al-Jinn as evidence. I do not accept it as established: jinn are called jinn because they are concealed, and people nās (ناس) because they are manifest, from īnās (إيناس), seeing, just as they are called bashar. Even if nās (ناس) applied to both classes, and that were sound and established, it would not suit the Qurʾān's eloquence and its freedom from affectation.","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":114,"ar":"(مِنَ الْجِنَّةِ وَالنَّاسِ) بيان للذي يوسوس، على أن الشيطان ضربان: جني وإنسي، كما قال (شياطين الإنس والجن) [ الأنعام: 112 ]، وعن أبى ذرّ رضي الله عنه قال لرجل: هل تعوّذت بالله من شيطان الإنس؟ ويجوز أن يكون (مِنَ) متعلقا بيوسوس، ومعناه: ابتداء الغاية، أي: يوسوس في صدورهم من جهة الجنّ ومن جهة الناس، وقيل: من الجنة والناس بيان للناس، وأن اسم الناس ينطلق على الجنة، واستدلوا (بنفر) و (رجال) في سورة الجن.\n\nوما أحقه؛ لأن الجن سموا (جنا) لاجتنانهم، والناس (ناسا) لظهورهم، من الإيناس وهو الإبصار، كما سموا بشرا؛ ولو كان يقع الناس على القبيلين، وصح ذلك وثبت: لم يكن مناسبا لفصاحة القرآن وبعده من التصنع","html":"<p>“From among jinn and humankind” explains “who whispers,” on the basis that Satan is of two kinds, jinn and human, as He says: “devils of humankind and jinn” [6:112]. Abū Dharr—may God be pleased with him—is reported to have asked a man: “Have you sought refuge in God from human Satan?”\nMin may also attach to yuwaswisu, denoting a starting point: that is, he whispers in their breasts from the direction of the jinn and from the direction of human beings.\nIt is also said that “from among jinn and humankind” explains al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>), and that the name al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>) applies to jinn. They adduce nafar (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">نفر</bdi>) [a group] and rijāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">رجال</bdi>) [men] in Sūrat al-Jinn as evidence. I do not accept it as established: jinn are called jinn because they are concealed, and people nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ناس</bdi>) because they are manifest, from īnās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إيناس</bdi>), seeing, just as they are called bashar. Even if nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ناس</bdi>) applied to both classes, and that were sound and established, it would not suit the Qurʾān&#39;s eloquence and its freedom from affectation.</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true,"display_normalization":"Reversed electronic reference brackets normalized; source text preserved in the dossier."}},{"id":"T114.06","heading":"[T114.06 | XVI655 | on K114.05]","text":"His words, “From the direction of human beings”: for example, he whispers into a Muslim's heart, concerning astrologers and soothsayers, that they know the unseen; and concerning jinn, that they harm and benefit.\nIn al-Muṭṭalaʿ: “According to one explanation, if the phrase is taken as clarification, ‘from among jinn and humankind’ is a circumstantial expression (ḥāl (حال)) relating to the pronoun in ‘who whispers.’”","title":"“From the direction of human beings”","author":"tibi","on":["K114.05"],"anchor":"K114.05","surah":114,"ar":"قولُه: (ومن جهة الناس)، مثلُ أن يوسوسَ في قلب المسلمِ من جهة المنجّمين والكُهانِ أنهم يعلمون الغيب، ومن جهةِ الجنِّ أنهم يَضرّون وينفعون. في \"المطلع\": «وعن بعضهم: على البيانِ يكونُ \"من الجِنّةِ والناس\"، حالًا من ضمير \"الذي يوسوس\"».","html":"<p>His words, “From the direction of human beings”: for example, he whispers into a Muslim&#39;s heart, concerning astrologers and soothsayers, that they know the unseen; and concerning jinn, that they harm and benefit.\nIn al-Muṭṭalaʿ: “According to one explanation, if the phrase is taken as clarification, ‘from among jinn and humankind’ is a circumstantial expression (ḥāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حال</bdi>)) relating to the pronoun in ‘who whispers.’”</p>","lemma":"ومن جهة الناس","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T114.07","heading":"[T114.07 | XVI655 | on K114.05]","text":"His words, “I do not accept it as established”: that is, I do not establish it, from their saying ḥaqaqtu al-shayʾa aḥuqquhu (حَقَقْتُ الشيءَ أَحُقُّه), “I established the thing.”\nThe Imam says: “It is said that ‘from among jinn and humankind’ gives two divisions included under ‘in the breasts of al-nās (الناس).’ It is as though the common element shared by jinn and humans were called insān (إنسان), while the human being were also called insān (إنسان); the expression insān (إنسان) would thus apply equivocally to both genus and species. The evidence is the report that a group of jinn came and were asked, ‘Who are you?’ They answered, ‘Nās from among the jinn.’ God has also called them rijāl (رجال) in ‘There were men of humankind who sought refuge with men of the jinn, and they increased them in distress’ [72:6]. Thus it would be permissible to call them nās (ناس) here. This strained statement—he does not mean that it is weak—for making insān (إنسان) the name of a genus containing both jinn and humans is remote from the language.”\n[SOURCE CHECK T114-U3: The earlier scan report gives وهذا القول المتعسف لا يريد أنه ضعيف. Al-Rāzī's tafsīr of 114:6 instead reads وهذا القول ضعيف, followed by the same linguistic objection. This confirms that the source criticizes the interpretation, but does not establish how the corruption arose in Ṭībī. The printed wording is retained with this warning; displacement from the next gloss remains a hypothesis. Mā in وما أحقه is negative, not exclamatory.]","title":"“I do not accept it as established”","author":"tibi","on":["K114.05"],"anchor":"K114.05","surah":114,"ar":"قولُه: (وما أحقّه)، يعني: ما أثبته من قولهم: حَقَقْتُ الشئَ أَحُقُّه، أي: أَثبتُه. قال الإمام: «قيل: إن قولَه: ﴿مِنَ الْجِنَّةِ وَالنَّاسِ﴾ قسمان مندرجان تحت قولِه: ﴿فِي صُدُورِ النَّاسِ﴾، كأن القدرَ المشتركَ بين الجنِّ والإنسِ سُمّي إنسانًا، والإنسانُ أيضًا سُمّي إنسانًا، فيكون لفظُ الإنسانِ واقعًا على الجنسِ والنوعِ بالاشتراك. والدليلُ عليه ما رُويَ أنه جاءَ نفرٌ من الجن، فقيل لهم: مَنْ أنتم؟ فقالوا: ناسٌ من الجن.\n\nوأيضًا قد سَمّاهم اللهُ رجالًا في قوله: ﴿وَأَنَّهُ كَانَ رِجَالٌ مِنَ الْإِنْسِ يَعُوذُونَ بِرِجَالٍ مِنَ الْجِنِّ فَزَادُوهُمْ رَهَقًا﴾ [الجن: 6]، فجازَ أن يُسميَهم هنا ناسًا. وهذا القولُ المتعسِّفُ لا يريدُ أنَه ضعيفٌ، لأن جَعْلَ الإنسانِ اسمًا للجنسِ الذي يندرجُ فيه الجنُّ والإنس، بعيدٌ من اللغة».","html":"<p>His words, “I do not accept it as established”: that is, I do not establish it, from their saying ḥaqaqtu al-shayʾa aḥuqquhu (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">حَقَقْتُ الشيءَ أَحُقُّه</bdi>), “I established the thing.”\nThe Imam says: “It is said that ‘from among jinn and humankind’ gives two divisions included under ‘in the breasts of al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>).’ It is as though the common element shared by jinn and humans were called insān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إنسان</bdi>), while the human being were also called insān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إنسان</bdi>); the expression insān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إنسان</bdi>) would thus apply equivocally to both genus and species. The evidence is the report that a group of jinn came and were asked, ‘Who are you?’ They answered, ‘Nās from among the jinn.’ God has also called them rijāl (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">رجال</bdi>) in ‘There were men of humankind who sought refuge with men of the jinn, and they increased them in distress’ [72:6]. Thus it would be permissible to call them nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ناس</bdi>) here. This strained statement—he does not mean that it is weak—for making insān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">إنسان</bdi>) the name of a genus containing both jinn and humans is remote from the language.”\n[SOURCE CHECK T114-U3: The earlier scan report gives <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وهذا القول المتعسف لا يريد أنه ضعيف</bdi>. Al-Rāzī&#39;s tafsīr of 114:6 instead reads <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وهذا القول ضعيف</bdi>, followed by the same linguistic objection. This confirms that the source criticizes the interpretation, but does not establish how the corruption arose in Ṭībī. The printed wording is retained with this warning; displacement from the next gloss remains a hypothesis. Mā in <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">وما أحقه</bdi> is negative, not exclamatory.]</p>","lemma":"وما أحقه","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"114:6","number":6,"ar":"مِنَ ٱلْجِنَّةِ وَٱلنَّاسِ","en":"Of the jinn and of mankind"}],"quran_ar":"مِنَ ٱلْجِنَّةِ وَٱلنَّاسِ ﴿6﴾","quran_en":"6. Of the jinn and of mankind"},{"id":"K114.06","q":"114:5–6","s":114,"surah":"al-Nās","surah_ar":"الناس","title":"al-Nās · Better than that is to understand al-nās as al-nāsī [the forgetful one], like “the day the caller ca","cairo":"K IV:823–825 · T XVI:652–656","source_page_first":823,"source_page_last":825,"chunk":"passages-114","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Text and explanation","html":"<p>Better than that is to understand al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>) as al-nāsī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناسي</bdi>) [the forgetful one], like “the day the caller calls” [54:6], and as “from where al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>) departed” [2:199] has been read. It is then explained by “jinn and humankind,” since the two weighty kinds (al-thaqalān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الثقلان</bdi>)) are the two species characterized by forgetting God&#39;s due—mighty and majestic is He.\nIt is related from the Messenger of God ﷺ: “Two sūrahs have been sent down to me the like of which has never been sent down. You will never recite two sūrahs more beloved or more pleasing to God than these”—meaning the two sūrahs of refuge. The two sūrahs of refuge are also called al-muqashqishatān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">المقشقشتان</bdi>).\n[TRANSLATOR&#39;S NOTE: The phonological argument is the omission of final yāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ي</bdi>) in al-nāsī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناسي</bdi>), parallel to al-dāʿī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الداعي</bdi>)/al-dāʿ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الداع</bdi>). No claim that the ordinary reading means “forgetful” is being added.]</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K114.06"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“Better than that”","html":"<p>His words, “Better than that”: that is, better than this strained statement; he does not mean that it is an interpretation possessing merit. It is to take al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>) in “the breasts of al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>)” as “the forgetful one.” It can then be divided into jinn and humans, for they are two classes characterized by forgetting God&#39;s due.\n[TEXT NOTE: The scan/transcription has <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صفتان موصوفان</bdi>, literally “two attributes described.” The contextual “two classes” follows the preceding discussion and needs verification against another witness.]</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T114.08"},{"type":"exposition","title":"“Al-muqashqishatān”","html":"<p>His words, “Al-muqashqishatān.” Al-Nihāya: “In the report, the sūrahs ‘Say: O unbelievers’ and ‘Say: He is God, One’ are called al-muqashqishatān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">المقشقشتان</bdi>): the two that free one from hypocrisy and association of partners with God, as a sick person is freed from his illness. One says taqashqasha al-marīḍ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">المريض</bdi>) when the sick person recovers and becomes well.”\n[TRANSLATOR&#39;S NOTE: The base text applies the term to al-Falaq and al-Nās; this quotation applies it to al-Kāfirūn and al-Ikhlāṣ. The differing identifications are preserved.]\nThe sūrah is complete.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T114.09"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"وأجود منه أن يراد بالناس: الناسي، كقوله: (يَوْمَ يَدْعُ الدَّاعِ) [ القمر: 6 ]كما قرئ: (مِنْ حَيْثُ أَفاضَ النَّاسُ) [ البقرة: 199 ]، ثم يبين بالجنة والناس؛ لأنّ الثقلين هما النوعان الموصوفان بنسيان حق الله عز وجل.\n\nعن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «لقد أنزلت علىّ سورتان ما أنزل مثلهما، وإنك لن تقرأ سورتين أحب ولا أرضى عند الله منهما» يعنى المعوذتين. ويقال للمعوذتين: المقشقشتان.\n\nقولُه: (وأجودُ منه)، أي: من هذا القولِ المتعسِّف: لا يريدُ أنه وجهٌ فيه جَوْدة، وهو أن يُحملَ \"الناسِ\" في قولِه: ﴿صُدُورِ النَّاسِ﴾ على الناسي، فحينئذٍ يمكنُ تقسيمُه إلى الجنِّ والإنسِ لأنهما صفتانِ موصوفانِ بنسيانِ حقِّ الله.\n\nقولُه: (المُقَشْقِشَتان)، النهاية: «في الحديثِ: يقالُ لسورتَيْ \"قُلْ يا أيها الكافرون\"، و\"قُلْ هو الله أحد\": المُقَشْقِشَتان، أي: المبرِّئتانِ من النفاقِ والشركِ، كما يَبرأُ المريضُ من عِلَّتِه؛ يقال: قد تَقَشْقشَ المريض: إذا أفاقَ وبَرَأ».\n\nتَمّتِ السُّورَة","layers":[{"id":"K114.06","heading":"[K114.06 | T XVI656; K IV824]","text":"Better than that is to understand al-nās (الناس) as al-nāsī (الناسي) [the forgetful one], like “the day the caller calls” [54:6], and as “from where al-nās (الناس) departed” [2:199] has been read. It is then explained by “jinn and humankind,” since the two weighty kinds (al-thaqalān (الثقلان)) are the two species characterized by forgetting God's due—mighty and majestic is He.\nIt is related from the Messenger of God ﷺ: “Two sūrahs have been sent down to me the like of which has never been sent down. You will never recite two sūrahs more beloved or more pleasing to God than these”—meaning the two sūrahs of refuge. The two sūrahs of refuge are also called al-muqashqishatān (المقشقشتان).\n[TRANSLATOR'S NOTE: The phonological argument is the omission of final yāʾ (ي) in al-nāsī (الناسي), parallel to al-dāʿī (الداعي)/al-dāʿ (الداع). No claim that the ordinary reading means “forgetful” is being added.]","title":"Text and explanation","author":"kashshaf","surah":114,"ar":"وأجود منه أن يراد بالناس: الناسي، كقوله: (يَوْمَ يَدْعُ الدَّاعِ) [ القمر: 6 ]كما قرئ: (مِنْ حَيْثُ أَفاضَ النَّاسُ) [ البقرة: 199 ]، ثم يبين بالجنة والناس؛ لأنّ الثقلين هما النوعان الموصوفان بنسيان حق الله عز وجل.\n\nعن رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم: «لقد أنزلت علىّ سورتان ما أنزل مثلهما، وإنك لن تقرأ سورتين أحب ولا أرضى عند الله منهما» يعنى المعوذتين. ويقال للمعوذتين: المقشقشتان.","html":"<p>Better than that is to understand al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>) as al-nāsī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناسي</bdi>) [the forgetful one], like “the day the caller calls” [54:6], and as “from where al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>) departed” [2:199] has been read. It is then explained by “jinn and humankind,” since the two weighty kinds (al-thaqalān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الثقلان</bdi>)) are the two species characterized by forgetting God&#39;s due—mighty and majestic is He.\nIt is related from the Messenger of God ﷺ: “Two sūrahs have been sent down to me the like of which has never been sent down. You will never recite two sūrahs more beloved or more pleasing to God than these”—meaning the two sūrahs of refuge. The two sūrahs of refuge are also called al-muqashqishatān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">المقشقشتان</bdi>).\n[TRANSLATOR&#39;S NOTE: The phonological argument is the omission of final yāʾ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">ي</bdi>) in al-nāsī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناسي</bdi>), parallel to al-dāʿī (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الداعي</bdi>)/al-dāʿ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الداع</bdi>). No claim that the ordinary reading means “forgetful” is being added.]</p>","lemma":"","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true,"display_normalization":"Reversed electronic reference brackets normalized; source text preserved in the dossier."}},{"id":"T114.08","heading":"[T114.08 | XVI656 | on K114.06]","text":"His words, “Better than that”: that is, better than this strained statement; he does not mean that it is an interpretation possessing merit. It is to take al-nās (الناس) in “the breasts of al-nās (الناس)” as “the forgetful one.” It can then be divided into jinn and humans, for they are two classes characterized by forgetting God's due.\n[TEXT NOTE: The scan/transcription has صفتان موصوفان, literally “two attributes described.” The contextual “two classes” follows the preceding discussion and needs verification against another witness.]","title":"“Better than that”","author":"tibi","on":["K114.06"],"anchor":"K114.06","surah":114,"ar":"قولُه: (وأجودُ منه)، أي: من هذا القولِ المتعسِّف: لا يريدُ أنه وجهٌ فيه جَوْدة، وهو أن يُحملَ \"الناسِ\" في قولِه: ﴿صُدُورِ النَّاسِ﴾ على الناسي، فحينئذٍ يمكنُ تقسيمُه إلى الجنِّ والإنسِ لأنهما صفتانِ موصوفانِ بنسيانِ حقِّ الله.","html":"<p>His words, “Better than that”: that is, better than this strained statement; he does not mean that it is an interpretation possessing merit. It is to take al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>) in “the breasts of al-nās (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">الناس</bdi>)” as “the forgetful one.” It can then be divided into jinn and humans, for they are two classes characterized by forgetting God&#39;s due.\n[TEXT NOTE: The scan/transcription has <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">صفتان موصوفان</bdi>, literally “two attributes described.” The contextual “two classes” follows the preceding discussion and needs verification against another witness.]</p>","lemma":"وأجود منه","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}},{"id":"T114.09","heading":"[T114.09 | XVI656 | on K114.06]","text":"His words, “Al-muqashqishatān.” Al-Nihāya: “In the report, the sūrahs ‘Say: O unbelievers’ and ‘Say: He is God, One’ are called al-muqashqishatān (المقشقشتان): the two that free one from hypocrisy and association of partners with God, as a sick person is freed from his illness. One says taqashqasha al-marīḍ (المريض) when the sick person recovers and becomes well.”\n[TRANSLATOR'S NOTE: The base text applies the term to al-Falaq and al-Nās; this quotation applies it to al-Kāfirūn and al-Ikhlāṣ. The differing identifications are preserved.]\nThe sūrah is complete.","title":"“Al-muqashqishatān”","author":"tibi","on":["K114.06"],"anchor":"K114.06","surah":114,"ar":"قولُه: (المُقَشْقِشَتان)، النهاية: «في الحديثِ: يقالُ لسورتَيْ \"قُلْ يا أيها الكافرون\"، و\"قُلْ هو الله أحد\": المُقَشْقِشَتان، أي: المبرِّئتانِ من النفاقِ والشركِ، كما يَبرأُ المريضُ من عِلَّتِه؛ يقال: قد تَقَشْقشَ المريض: إذا أفاقَ وبَرَأ».\n\nتَمّتِ السُّورَة","html":"<p>His words, “Al-muqashqishatān.” Al-Nihāya: “In the report, the sūrahs ‘Say: O unbelievers’ and ‘Say: He is God, One’ are called al-muqashqishatān (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">المقشقشتان</bdi>): the two that free one from hypocrisy and association of partners with God, as a sick person is freed from his illness. One says taqashqasha al-marīḍ (<bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">المريض</bdi>) when the sick person recovers and becomes well.”\n[TRANSLATOR&#39;S NOTE: The base text applies the term to al-Falaq and al-Nās; this quotation applies it to al-Kāfirūn and al-Ikhlāṣ. The differing identifications are preserved.]\nThe sūrah is complete.</p>","lemma":"المقشقشتان","review":{"electronic_comparison":"completed","new_scan_collation":false,"final_scholarly_approval":false,"dossier":"reviews/final-surahs.json","arabic_complete":true}}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"114:5","number":5,"ar":"ٱلَّذِى يُوَسْوِسُ فِى صُدُورِ ٱلنَّاسِ","en":"Who whispereth in the hearts of mankind"},{"key":"114:6","number":6,"ar":"مِنَ ٱلْجِنَّةِ وَٱلنَّاسِ","en":"Of the jinn and of mankind"}],"quran_ar":"ٱلَّذِى يُوَسْوِسُ فِى صُدُورِ ٱلنَّاسِ ﴿5﴾ مِنَ ٱلْجِنَّةِ وَٱلنَّاسِ ﴿6﴾","quran_en":"5. Who whispereth in the hearts of mankind 6. Of the jinn and of mankind"}];
